#: processes/sdlc/code-change-review.bpmn:225
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Every finding is answered — implemented, or replied to with why not. A design question does not excuse skipping the nits in the same review."
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-change-review.bpmn:109
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Exists as a separate lane for one reason: what it tests — the merge of head into base — is not a claim the Agent lane could make about its own tree, so GW_CIGreen has to be fed by an independent run rather than by the agent reporting its own gates as done."
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-change-review.bpmn:339
#. Diagram label
msgid "findings"
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-change-review.bpmn:45
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Getting a change to the PLATFORM from \"this is needed\" to merged: claim the work, branch and announce it, make the change, prove it against the platform's own gates, and carry it through review to a merge somebody else is not left holding. You are in this process for a change to folio-assistant itself. `content-change-review` is the same shape for a folio's content, and the acceptance differs: here a code reviewer judges the engineering, there a review committee judges the editorial. The loop back through \"root-cause the failure\" is the point of drawing this at all. Red CI is not a state you report and leave; it is a state you re-enter the process from. The same is true of a review that is not clear."
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-change-review.bpmn:335
#. Diagram label
msgid "green"
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-change-review.bpmn:113
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Its verdict, not the agent's own account of the diff, is what GW_ReviewClear reads: a finding routes the change back through Task_AddressReview to Task_Implement, and only a clear routes it forward to Task_PrepareMerge, so this lane alone determines which of those two paths a change takes."
msgstr ""

